\section{A Taxonomy of Randomness Failures}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

We organize failures by \emph{where in the generator} the fault occurs. For the generator itself
we adopt the decomposition of the NIST SP 800-90 series rather than proposing a new one
(Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}). The entropy source and its conditioning (\stage{1}, \stage{2})
correspond to SP 800-90B~\cite{nist80090b}. The deterministic generator, its instantiation, and
its reseeding and internal state (\stage{3} to \stage{5}) correspond to the DRBG mechanisms of
SP 800-90A~\cite{nist80090a}. SP 800-90C specifies how validated components are assembled into a
complete random bit generator~\cite{nist80090c}. Our contribution at this level is limited to
two loci that the series leaves out and that recur in the deployed record. The first is
consumption (\stage{6}): how a protocol uses the values it receives, which is where signature
nonces fail. The second is the build and integration surface (\stage{$\ast$}): SP 800-90 concerns
the design of each component and of their composition, whereas whether a shipped artifact
actually invokes the validated components is decided by the build, which lies outside its scope.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/taxonomy_pipeline.pdf}
\caption{The randomness pipeline. Stages \stage{1} to \stage{5} follow the NIST SP 800-90
series, as the bands above them show. Consumption (\stage{6}) and the cross-cutting build and
integration surface (\stage{$\ast$}) lie outside it. Under each stage are corpus incidents whose
primary cause lies there (Table~\ref{tab:incidents}). \stage{$\ast$} can replace the component
at any stage without changing the stage's own design.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The six pipeline stages}
We take the stages in order, from the physical origin of unpredictability to its consumption at
the cryptographic interface. Each plays a distinct role in turning raw noise into usable key
material, and each fails in a characteristic way that the rest of the paper ties to concrete
incidents.

\textbf{\stage{1} Entropy source.} The physical or operating-system origin of unpredictability.
Failures here are quantitative: no source is present, the source is biased or low-rate, its
output is environmentally predictable, or, most commonly in the field, insufficient entropy has
accumulated at the moment of first use. Embedded and headless devices that generate keys
seconds after boot, before interrupts and disk timings have stirred the pool, are the recurring
victims~\cite{heninger2012mining,everspaugh2014whirlwind}. The source can also simply be broken
in silicon: AMD disclosed in 2025 that the 16-bit and 32-bit forms of the \texttt{RDSEED}
instruction on Zen 5 processors return zero at a rate inconsistent with randomness while
incorrectly signaling success, so software that trusts the success flag consumes a fixed value
believing it to be fresh entropy~\cite{amd2025rdseed}.

\textbf{\stage{2} Accumulation and conditioning.} Raw noise must be collected, mixed,
health-tested, and its entropy estimated before use. Failures include the absence of on-line
health tests, over-estimation of the entropy actually gathered, and the fallacy that hashing or
whitening adds entropy. Conditioning changes the \emph{statistics} of a value but never enlarges
the set of values it can take~\cite{rfc4086,aumasson2024serious}.

\textbf{\stage{3} Generation algorithm.} The deterministic core that stretches a seed into
output. Failures are qualitative: a non-cryptographic generator is used where a CSPRNG is
required, such as a linear congruential generator, the Mersenne Twister of Milk Sad, or the LFSR
of MIFARE Crypto-1. The algorithm may also contain a deliberate back door (\dualec{}), or a
standardized algorithm may admit state recovery under realistic side channels, as cache attacks
on CTR\_DRBG showed~\cite{milksad2023,garcia2008mifare,bernstein2016dualec,cohney2020blackswan}.

\textbf{\stage{4} Seeding and instantiation.} A sound DRBG is only as strong as the entropy that
instantiates it. Failures include predictable seeds derived from a process identifier and clock,
low-entropy seeds of a few bits, static or hardcoded seeds, seeds shorter than the security
level, and identical seeds shipped across a device fleet~\cite{goldberg1996netscape,yilek2009debian,cohney2018duhk}.

\textbf{\stage{5} State lifecycle.} Once running, a generator must reseed, resist state
compromise, and never repeat state. Failures include never reseeding, cloning state when a
virtual machine is reset or snapshotted, restoring a persisted state file, forward-security gaps,
and reseed operations that inject too few bits to matter. Resetting a virtual machine to a
snapshot replays the generator state, so two sessions reuse the same randomness, and a forked
process that does not reseed does the same inside a
library~\cite{ristenpart2010vmreset,strenzke2016openssl}.

\textbf{\stage{6} Consumption.} Even a perfect generator fails if the consumer misuses it. The
dominant case is the signature nonce: ECDSA and DSA leak the private key if the per-signature
nonce repeats or is even slightly biased, and this has recurred from the PlayStation~3 to
production cryptocurrency wallets~\cite{fail0verflow2010ps3,bos2014ecc,breitner2019biased}. IV
reuse, requesting fewer random bytes than a primitive needs, and insecure API defaults belong
here too. This stage also hosts a failure mode with the opposite character, in which the nonce is
not weak at all but chosen adversarially to encode a secret, turning a random-looking field into
an exfiltration channel. We treat it separately in Section~\ref{sec:covert} because it defeats
every defense aimed at entropy quality.

\subsection{The cross-cutting build and integration surface}
Real systems are assembled: sources are selected by build flags, algorithms are pulled from
dependencies, and the wiring between them is decided by configuration and toolchains. We treat
this surface, \stage{$\ast$}, as a failure locus of its own. Its failure modes are a symbol
collision that resolves a call to the wrong implementation, a build flag that disables the
intended source without stopping the build, a downstream patch that removes an entropy input,
and a substituted constant or component. Its defining property is that no stage is individually
wrong, yet the shipped artifact is broken, so review and testing of each component pass. Three
corpus incidents sit here. The 2008 Debian patch deleted the line that mixed entropy into
OpenSSL's pool~\cite{yilek2009debian}. In Juniper ScreenOS, an unauthorized code change replaced
the \dualec{} constant and a coding error exposed raw \dualec{} output~\cite{checkoway2016juniper}.
In \coldcard{}, a symbol collision linked seed generation to a software
generator~\cite{coinkite2026backgrounder}. The surface overlaps with software supply-chain
security, but its consequence for randomness is specific: the substitution changes no interface
and no output statistic, so it survives the checks aimed at the internal stages
(Section~\ref{sec:blindspot}).

\subsection{Accidental versus induced failure}
\label{sec:induced}
Most corpus incidents are accidental. Every stage can also be driven into failure deliberately,
and laboratory work shows how, although such demonstrations are outside our corpus of deployed
incidents. Mart{\'i}n \etal{}~\cite{martin2015strng} show that clock glitches, temperature
manipulation, and underpowering measurably reduce the randomness of true random number
generators, and Bayon \etal{}~\cite{bayon2012em} lock the oscillators of a ring-oscillator
generator with contactless electromagnetic injection. Both are \stage{1} attacks by a local (A2)
adversary. Mavroudis \etal{}~\cite{mavroudis2017touch} start from the premise that individual
cryptographic chips may be subverted (A3) and build systems whose security survives a minority
of bad components. Induced and accidental failures land on the same stages and are exploited
the same way, so one taxonomy serves both. The difference is in the defenses: fault and
supply-chain resistance need physical countermeasures that code review cannot supply.

\subsection{Coding dimensions}
\label{sec:coding}
Each incident is coded on the dimensions of Table~\ref{tab:dimensions}. Each dimension answers
one question, and the later tables reuse the same codes, so an incident can be followed from
Table~\ref{tab:incidents} to the coverage matrix of Section~\ref{sec:effectiveness}.

